menuconfig UBI
        bool "Unsorted Block Images (UBI)"
        select FLASH
        select FLASH_MAP
        select CRC
        select PSA_WANT_ALG_CMAC
        select PSA_WANT_ALG_HKDF
        select PSA_WANT_ALG_SHA_256
        select PSA_WANT_KEY_TYPE_AES
        select PSA_WANT_KEY_TYPE_DERIVE
        help
          Volume manager for raw flash. Maps logical erase blocks onto
          physical ones, spreads wear across the partition, survives power
          loss and authenticates its own metadata with AES-CMAC.

          UBI never encrypts or authenticates application data; only its own
          headers and volume table are protected.

if UBI

config UBI_MAX_NR_OF_VOLUMES
        int "Maximum number of volumes"
        default 4
        range 1 64
        help
          The only size limit that does not follow from the partition itself:
          how many logical erase blocks exist is decided by the partition,
          but how many volumes they are divided into is a design choice of
          the application.

config UBI_VERIFY_ON_READ
        bool "Verify the VID header before every read"
        default n
        help
          Re-reads and authenticates the header on every ubi_leb_read().
          Headers are always verified during attach, so this only guards
          against tampering while the device is running, and it costs an
          extra flash read plus a CMAC on the hottest path.

config UBI_PROTECTION_CYCLES
        int "Erases a freshly handed out block is left alone for"
        default 64
        range 0 255
        help
          A block is given out because it was the least worn one free, which
          makes it the least worn one in use the moment something is written
          to it, and so the first thing wear levelling would want to move.
          Moving it would be wasted work: the caller has only just filled it
          and may be about to rewrite it anyway.

          This counts erases anywhere on the device, not time. Zero turns
          the protection off. The count lives in RAM, so an attach starts
          every block unprotected.

          Too small is the dangerous direction. The window has to outlast
          how often the hottest data is rewritten, or levelling carries a
          block off just before the caller replaces it anyway. Measured on
          a pool of worn blocks flooded with barely used ones, a window of
          8 left every one of 4096 rewrites being carried off and doubled
          the erase count; 16 and above stopped it dead. Linux uses 10, but
          its levelling threshold is 4096 against our 256, so it reaches
          for a block far less often.

config UBI_SELF_CHECKS
        bool "Check the library's own choices at runtime"
        default n
        help
          Some of what UBI decides cannot be seen from outside: which block
          it allocated and which one it moved data off are both answers to
          "the least worn one", and the only evidence a caller gets is an
          erase counter that went up. This turns those decisions into
          statements the library checks against itself.

          Each check is a second pass over the block table, so it doubles
          the cost of a choice that is already the expensive part of an
          allocation. Meant for tests and for bringing up a new part, not
          for shipping.

config UBI_STATE_CHECK_INTERVAL
        int "Flash writes between state checks"
        default 64
        range 1 1024
        help
          UBI counts every write it makes to the flash. Once this many have
          gone by, the state callback is consulted before the next operation
          that would write, so that a refusal can be honoured with nothing
          written.

          The callback is always consulted at the end of an attach; this only
          decides how often it is consulted afterwards, so that a long uptime
          is not a way around the check. It does no flash I/O, so a small
          number is cheap.

config UBI_WEAR_LEVELING_THRESHOLD
        int "Erase count spread that makes relocation worthwhile"
        default 256
        range 64 512
        help
          Reported through ubi_device_get_info(); a spread wider than this
          means UBI_MAINTENANCE_RELOCATE has work to do.

module = UBI
module-str = ubi
source "subsys/logging/Kconfig.template.log_config"

endif # UBI
